 filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $36\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(36\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(36\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =30-\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q=30.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(13+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|13+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(13+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(13+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q) & =(13+t)^{4}\\cdot 30 \\\\ (13+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(13+t)^{3}Q & =30(13+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4}) & =30(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 30(13+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(13+t)^{4} & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(13+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6(13+t)+\\frac{C}{(13+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $39\\,\\text{lb}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=39,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =39 \\\\ 6(13+0)+\\frac{C}{(13+0)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ 78+\\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =-39 \\\\ C & =-39(13)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6(13+t)-\\frac{39(13)^{4}}{(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =6(13+t)-39{(\\frac{13}{13+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $342\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=342$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =342 \\\\ 117+45{t}_{o}-36{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 117+9{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 9{t}_{o} & =225 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =6(13+25)-39{(\\frac{13}{13+25})}^{4} \\\\  & =228-39{(\\frac{13}{38})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈227.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186539460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539418 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186539460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186539460 #edb/ref 1071 "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $306\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $234\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $78\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186539460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fbffe068-df49-5949-a93c-dd6aa67cd689" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186539460 #edb/